WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:05.85
This is journey twelve of Hewn, the software factory: review the backlog.

00:00:05.85 --> 00:00:10.45
After stage nine estimates a product, Hewn builds it one increment at a time.

00:00:10.45 --> 00:00:15.40
Owners and Viewers want to see that plan: what comes next, what is being built now,

00:00:15.40 --> 00:00:18.20
what has merged, and what was left out and why.

00:00:18.20 --> 00:00:23.03
This video walks through how it was built: the backlog endpoint, the Backlog tab,

00:00:23.03 --> 00:00:27.21
the deferred designs, blocked increments, live updates, and the tests.

00:00:28.36 --> 00:00:32.86
A user opens a project's Backlog and sees the increments in order,

00:00:32.86 --> 00:00:38.53
each with its status, its estimate, and a link to its pull request once one exists.

00:00:38.53 --> 00:00:43.73
Deferred designs appear apart from the active backlog, each with its reason.

00:00:43.73 --> 00:00:45.71
A blocked increment says why.

00:00:45.71 --> 00:00:50.01
And percent complete moves after every merge, without a reload.

00:00:51.16 --> 00:00:52.34
Here is the flow.

00:00:52.34 --> 00:00:56.16
The Backlog tab asks the API for the project's backlog.

00:00:56.16 --> 00:01:00.87
When an increment changes, the project page already hears the event,

00:01:00.87 --> 00:01:03.79
so the tab simply reads the backlog again.

00:01:04.94 --> 00:01:10.16
The increments table has existed since stage nine was built: the worker records the

00:01:10.16 --> 00:01:13.94
plan there, and stage ten reports each increment's progress.

00:01:13.94 --> 00:01:16.33
What was missing was a way to read it.

00:01:16.33 --> 00:01:20.11
The new backlog query takes the project's current direction,

00:01:20.11 --> 00:01:24.89
the same one Projects describes, and returns its increments in number order,

00:01:24.89 --> 00:01:27.98
with each pull request turned into a GitHub link.

00:01:28.33 --> 00:01:33.73
Deferred designs live in the same table without a number, so they come back apart,

00:01:33.73 --> 00:01:34.98
with their reasons.

00:01:34.98 --> 00:01:38.27
The totals count merged increments and ideal days,

00:01:38.27 --> 00:01:42.94
and percent complete comes from the same reader the Projects list uses,

00:01:42.94 --> 00:01:44.52
so the two always agree.

00:01:45.67 --> 00:01:50.98
In Mission Control, the project header gained section tabs, Pipeline and Backlog,

00:01:50.98 --> 00:01:52.16
as the mocks show.

00:01:52.16 --> 00:01:56.49
The Backlog is a tab of the project page, so it shares the header,

00:01:56.49 --> 00:01:58.92
the controls and the live connection.

00:01:59.27 --> 00:02:04.17
Each status reads with its own label and icon, never colour alone: Queued,

00:02:04.17 --> 00:02:06.29
In progress, Blocked and Merged.

00:02:06.29 --> 00:02:10.07
The pull request column shows the link once there is one,

00:02:10.07 --> 00:02:13.05
and "soon" while an increment is being built.

00:02:14.20 --> 00:02:19.20
When an increment uses up its ten implement-and-test iterations, it is blocked,

00:02:19.20 --> 00:02:23.38
and Hewn moves on to the next increment that doesn't depend on it.

00:02:23.38 --> 00:02:28.63
The callout says exactly that: how many iterations it used, which tests still fail,

00:02:28.63 --> 00:02:33.38
and which increment Hewn moved on to, skipping anything that depends on it,

00:02:33.38 --> 00:02:35.15
directly or through another.

00:02:36.30 --> 00:02:40.02
The project page already follows the project's events.

00:02:40.02 --> 00:02:45.40
Every backlog plan, increment change, stage change or direction change bumps a

00:02:45.40 --> 00:02:50.50
counter that the Backlog tab watches, and the tab reads the backlog again.

00:02:50.50 --> 00:02:55.12
It costs one small read per event, and the totals are always right.

00:02:56.27 --> 00:03:00.16
To show deferred and blocked increments through the real pipeline,

00:03:00.16 --> 00:03:04.65
the scripted agent, which stands in for Claude in tests, learned two things.

00:03:04.65 --> 00:03:08.07
An idea's "Nice to have" sentence now goes into the brief,

00:03:08.07 --> 00:03:10.67
so stage nine's descoper defers that design,

00:03:10.67 --> 00:03:13.86
with the reason "Listed as nice-to-have in the brief".

00:03:13.86 --> 00:03:17.40
And a test can name an increment the agent never implements,

00:03:17.40 --> 00:03:19.64
so its tests stay red until it blocks.

00:03:19.64 --> 00:03:23.89
A third option slows each increment down, so a test can watch it change.

00:03:25.04 --> 00:03:28.53
The backend tests came first, and failed with not found.

00:03:28.53 --> 00:03:33.69
Now four tests cover the order, statuses, estimates and pull request links with the

00:03:33.69 --> 00:03:36.74
deferred designs, the totals moving with a merge,

00:03:36.74 --> 00:03:39.91
a blocked increment's iterations and failing tests,

00:03:39.91 --> 00:03:42.40
and the empty backlog before stage nine.

00:03:42.40 --> 00:03:45.64
The worker gained four tests for the scripted agent,

00:03:45.64 --> 00:03:48.44
and the frontend six for statuses, estimates,

00:03:48.44 --> 00:03:51.11
pull request links and the blocked callout.

00:03:51.46 --> 00:03:54.77
All green, the backend has two hundred and six tests,

00:03:54.77 --> 00:03:59.52
the worker one hundred and forty four, and the frontend one hundred and ten.

00:03:59.52 --> 00:04:03.84
End to end, there are thirteen new tests: five for the page's states,

00:04:03.84 --> 00:04:08.03
three visual comparisons with the mocks, four accessibility checks,

00:04:08.03 --> 00:04:13.28
and one with a real worker, where the backlog fills and every increment goes from In

00:04:13.28 --> 00:04:15.53
progress to Merged without a reload.

00:04:15.53 --> 00:04:20.71
Matching the blocked callout to its mock also fixed the visual helper: it now moves

00:04:20.71 --> 00:04:23.78
a region onto the pixel grid before comparing it.

00:04:24.93 --> 00:04:29.10
Here is Kiln Booking's Backlog, open before stage nine has run.

00:04:29.10 --> 00:04:30.49
Then a worker starts.

00:04:30.49 --> 00:04:35.33
The backlog arrives in journey order, with review history deferred below,

00:04:35.33 --> 00:04:37.72
listed as nice-to-have in the brief.

00:04:37.72 --> 00:04:42.49
The first increment goes In progress, then Merged with its pull request,

00:04:42.49 --> 00:04:46.13
and the merged count and percent complete move with it.

00:04:46.13 --> 00:04:47.33
Then the next one.

00:04:47.68 --> 00:04:51.57
When every increment has merged, the backlog reads two of two,

00:04:51.57 --> 00:04:53.01
at one hundred percent.

00:04:53.01 --> 00:04:55.33
It works in dark mode and on a phone.

00:04:55.68 --> 00:05:00.36
And here is Parcel Post, whose main workflow increment never went green.

00:05:00.36 --> 00:05:05.82
It is blocked after ten iterations, and the callout names the test that still fails.

00:05:06.97 --> 00:05:08.80
Journey twelve is complete.

00:05:08.80 --> 00:05:12.88
The Backlog shows every increment in order, with its status,

00:05:12.88 --> 00:05:14.65
estimate and pull request.

00:05:14.65 --> 00:05:18.05
Deferred designs appear apart, with their reasons.

00:05:18.05 --> 00:05:20.02
A blocked increment says why.

00:05:20.02 --> 00:05:22.94
And the totals move with every merge, live.

00:05:22.94 --> 00:05:24.85
Next comes journey thirteen.
